  "published": "2026-11-07T09:29:49.593Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "dotskit upgrade brings a solution of any 2.x release to the current one and leaves it building.\n\n- From any release since 2.0.0 one leap takes a solution to the current; --to stops at a release on the way, and a boundary of major versions crosses a step at a time.\n- A solution the team has not touched where the template owns the files upgrades without a single conflict: the merge settles silently, the way a git merge of branches does.\n- The merge runs by git on the branch dotskit/template: what conflicts is left in the files for the IDE or git mergetool, resolved the way the team resolves any merge, a part at a time, with the team's lines kept; --conflict-style zdiff3 adds the base of a file.\n- After the merge the solution builds: the references and usings of the types the template moved are repaired - from the text before the build, by the build after it.\n- An upgrade goes a part a run - the shared files first, then a service at a time, so the team resolves one part before the next; --all-at-once merges everything in one run. A step takes 51 s instead of 114 s.\n- The template writes .dotskit/manifest.json when it generates a solution, so dotskit new and dotskit upgrade work from the first run; dotskit init names the version of a solution made by the template alone, and the way from 1.x through 2.0.0.\n- --force follows the template and names every file where the team's lines gave way; it refuses --allow-dirty. A file the solution's git ignores is never written; a project the team took out of All.sln stays out.\n\nHow to update a solution generated from an earlier version: https://github.com/sawking-tech/DotNetSolutionKit/blob/v2.9.0/docs/getting-started/upgrading.md",
